  existing username from the [3mremote[0m payload in favor of a session id unless you want usernames queryable in your logs —
  tell me if you do, since that's a genuine debugging convenience with a genuine privacy cost.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should the logger enforce about data reaching the remote sink? → Allowlist for remote (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246m· Should usernames be queryable in shipped logs? → No, session id only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Two last things before I present the design.

  The repo has no linter, no formatter, and no test setup. A logger is exactly the kind of code that needs tests — the
  allowlist is a security control, and an untested security control is a hope. Tooling is also cheapest to add now,
  before there's code to retrofit.

  I also want to raise [1mglobal error capture[0m: hooking [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m / [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m in the browser and
  [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m / [38;5;153munhandledRejection[39m in Node. This is where most of the "debug production issues" value actually
  comes from — errors nobody thought to log. It's the difference between logging what you predicted and logging what
  actually broke. I recommend it, but it changes the volume and shape of what reaches your collector, so it's your call.

